 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Probably only a little better, and the air will likely still be unhealthy.\n\n**Tomorrow (Sat, 17 Oct):** The National Environment Agency (NEA) forecasts a 24-hour PSI of 150 – 230 (Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy). That's about the same as today's levels. As at 8pm, the 1-hr PM2.5 readings were 44 – 148 µg/m3 (Band 1, Normal / Band 2, Elevated), and the 24-hr PSI was 159 – 222, in the Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy range.\n\n**What could help:** Some showers are expected over Singapore and the surrounding region, including over parts of Kalimantan. With the winds forecast to shift to blow from the southeast or south-southeast, the transport of smoke haze from Kalimantan to Singapore may be reduced. However, some smoke haze from Sumatra may drift towards Singapore.\n\n**Further ahead:** Sunday and Monday look somewhat better. Showers are forecast over Singapore and parts of Kalimantan. Winds are expected to gradually weaken and blow from the southeast or south, which could further decrease the transport of smoke haze from Kalimantan. Smoke from Sumatra is still a risk on those days.\n\n**Health advice for tomorrow:**\n- **Healthy adults:** avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor physical exertion.\n- **Elderly, pregnant women and children:** minimise outdoor activity.\n- **People with chronic lung or heart disease:** avoid outdoor activity.\n\nThe forecast tells you what to plan for. For deciding what to do right now, check the hourly PM2.5 readings on the NEA website or the myENV app, because conditions can change quickly when showers come through.",
